<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minoan eruption</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">17th–16th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a> · Also known as Theran eruption, Thera eruption</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Minoan eruption was a catastrophic eruption of the Thera volcano on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> island of Santorini about 1600 BCE, one of the largest volcanic events in human history, which buried the settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akrotiri-prehistoric-city">Akrotiri</a> in pumice and ash. It occurred late in the Late Minoan IA phase, but its date is debated: radiocarbon dating has favoured the late 17th century BCE and archaeological links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> about 1550–1480 BCE. Earthquakes and tsunamis struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, where Minoan society recovered quickly, though the eruption's longer-term effects are disputed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minoan-eruption.jpg" alt="Minoan eruption" width="320" height="331" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santorini_ASTER.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
